    "rich_text_6aeaLY": {
      "type": "rich_text",
      "name": "Rich Text",
      "settings": {
        "richtext_inhalt": "<h3>WHAT DICOTA OFFERS AS A SUSTAINABLE PARTNER</h3><p><strong>RECYCLED MATERIALS – DOCUMENTED AND VERIFIABLE</strong></p><p>A growing proportion of the DICOTA portfolio consists of products made from recycled materials – in particular rPET (recycled polyethylene terephthalate), which is derived from plastic bottles. These products comply with GRS (Global Recycled Standard) certification and can be reported in the customer’s sustainability report as a documented contribution to the circular economy.</p><p>For resellers, this is a concrete selling point in sales discussions: “The sleeve used by each of your 500 employees is made from recycled material and is GRS-certified. You can report this in your sustainability report.” This is not an abstract ESG promise – it is a measurable, documentable contribution.</p><p><br/><strong>CERTIFICATIONS – THE PROOF, NOT THE PROMISE</strong></p><p>Sustainability claims without certification are worthless – procurement departments and CSR managers know this only too well. DICOTA relies on recognised, external certifications: GRS (Global Recycled Standard), Bluesign for responsible textile production, and RoHS compliance for low-toxicity manufacturing.</p><p>All certificates are available digitally and can be accessed at product level – not as a generic company document, but specific to each individual product. This is crucial for tenders and supplier assessments that require product-specific evidence.</p><p><strong><br/>CO₂ DATA FOR SCOPE 3 REPORTING</strong></p><p>The CSRD Directive requires companies subject to reporting obligations to record Scope 3 emissions – that is, emissions generated throughout the entire supply chain, not just within their own operations. IT accessories fall into this category: if a company purchases 1,000 laptop cases, this generates emissions – and these must increasingly be accounted for.</p><p>DICOTA is working on providing product-specific CO₂ data that retailers can pass on to their customers. For resellers able to offer this service, this creates a clear competitive advantage over suppliers lacking this level of transparency.</p><h3><br/>ENGAGING WITH THE RIGHT CONTACTS</h3><p><strong>CSR & SUSTAINABILITY OFFICERS</strong></p><p>This role is increasingly becoming a standalone function in medium-sized and large companies. The sustainability officer is responsible for ESG reporting and influences supplier assessment. This is a new point of contact for resellers – not an IT person, but someone with a focus on sustainability.</p><p>The conversation doesn’t work with product presentations, but with documentation and evidence. The opening line: \"I’d like to show you which certificates and sustainability data we can provide for our DICOTA products – this should be relevant for your next sustainability report.\"</p><p><strong><br/>STRATEGIC PROCUREMENT</strong></p><p>Many companies have incorporated ESG criteria into their supplier assessment – as a separate dimension on the scorecard alongside price, quality and delivery capability. This means that a supplier with a poor ESG rating loses points, even if they offer competitive prices. A supplier with excellent documentation gains points – and can therefore afford to offer price discounts.</p>",
        "width": "narrow",
        "padding_top": 4,
        "padding_bottom": 4
      }
    },
